I think you're missing the normal connotations of the words "spoiled"
and "spoilers". The reason they're called spoilers is that they spoil
the enjoyment of figuring things out for yourself. So if you've written
a huge stack of notes on the game yourself, they're not spoilers for
you (although they're spoilers for anyone else who might read them).

This also implies that it's impossible to spoil someone who's already
figured things out for themselves. (This is uncontroversial in the case
of things like TV shows; anyone who's watched an entire series cannot,
by definition, be spoiled on it. Why should it be any different for
games?)

